Hereby too there is a special effect (vaiśeṣika kārya) of the chief prāṇa — for the reason that he is taught (vyapadiśyate) in the śrutis as having five functions: ‘prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna’ (Bṛh. Up. 1.5.3). And this division of function presupposes a difference of effects: 
— prāṇa, with its forward operation (prāg-vṛtti), has out-breathing (ucchvāsa) and the like as its action; 
— apāna, with its downward operation (arvāg-vṛtti), has in-breathing (niśvāsa) and the like as its action; 
— vyāna, abiding at the conjunction of these two, is the cause of works of vigour (vīryavat-karma); 
— udāna, with upward operation (ūrdhva-vṛtti), is the cause of going-forth at death (utkrānti) and the like; 
— samāna conveys the food-essences (anna-rasān) equally throughout all limbs. 
Thus prāṇa is of five functions, like manas — that is, just as manas has five functions, so too prāṇa. The five well-known functions of manas are objects of sound and the rest, occasioned by the ear, etc. The five enumerated as ‘desire, resolution’ etc. are not to be taken, since they exceed the number five. 

Objection: Even here, mind has another function — namely an apprehension independent of the ear and the rest, having past and future and so forth as its objects; thus likewise the surplus over five is unavoidable. 

Reply: If so, then by the maxim ‘That which is not contradicted in another’s view is approved’ (paramatam apratiṣiddham anumataṃ bhavati), here too the five functions of manas well known in the Yoga-śāstra are taken in — namely ‘pramāṇa, viparyaya, vikalpa, nidrā, smṛtayaḥ’ (Pā. Yo. Sū. 1.6). Or it should be regarded that manas is shown as the illustration for prāṇa simply by way of having many functions (bahu-vṛtti-tva-mātra). Or this should be construed thus: prāṇa’s being an instrument of the jīva is also similar to manas’s, on account of its having five functions. (12)
